## RESULT (ALERT) DATA SCHEMA

Every Sandfly result has this header structure:

  {
    "exec_seconds": 0,
    "start_time":   "2026-03-13T10:00:00Z",  // RFC3339 scan start time
    "end_time":     "2026-03-13T10:00:01Z",  // RFC3339 scan end time
    "name":         "process_deleted",        // sandfly module name
    "status":       "alert",                  // "alert" | "pass" | "error"
    "status_msg":   "ok",
    "severity":     4,                        // 1=low ... 5=critical
    "tags": [                                 // MITRE ATT&CK + category tags
      "attack.id.T1070.004",
      "attack.tactic.defense_evasion",
      "default_active",
      "process"
    ],
    "type":     "process",                    // sandfly type (see types below)
    "engine":   "sandfly_engine_process",     // internal engine used
    "key_data": "/tmp/malware",               // primary forensic indicator
    "results": { ... }                        // type-specific forensic data
  }

### Sandfly Types and their results object

  process     — results.process: {name, pid, ppid, path, cmdline, hash.sha1/sha256/sha512,
                                   date.created_minutes, uid, username, deleted, env, ...}
  file        — results.file: {path, name, mode, uid, username, hash.md5/sha1/sha256/sha512,
                                date.{created,modified,accessed}_minutes, size, ...}
  directory   — results.directory: {path, name, mode, uid, username, inode, count, ...}
  user        — results.user: {name, uid, gid, home, shell, groups, password_type, ...}
  log         — results.log / results.file: log file forensic data
  cron        — results.cron: {user, command, schedule, ...}
  kernel      — results.kernel: {module name, taint flags, ...}
  systemd     — results.systemd: {unit name, state, ...}

### Severity scale
  1 = Informational (recon data, always_pass sandflies)
  2 = Low
  3 = Medium (suspicious, investigate)
  4 = High (likely malicious activity)
  5 = Critical (confirmed attack technique / rootkit indicator)

### MITRE ATT&CK tags
  Sandfly tags alerts with ATT&CK technique IDs and tactics.
  Common patterns:
    attack.id.T1070.004    — Indicator Removal: File Deletion
    attack.id.T1564.001    — Hide Artifacts: Hidden Files and Directories
    attack.id.T1059        — Command and Scripting Interpreter
    attack.id.T1098.004    — Account Manipulation: SSH Authorized Keys
    attack.tactic.defense_evasion
    attack.tactic.persistence
    attack.tactic.execution
    attack.tactic.credential_access
    attack.tactic.lateral_movement
    attack.tactic.discovery

### Key sandfly module names to know
  process_deleted                  — Process running from deleted binary (malware hiding in memory)
  process_running_from_tmp         — Process running from /tmp or other world-writable path
  process_running_hidden_parent    — Process with hidden parent PID (rootkit indicator)
  process_masquerading             — Process name spoofing legitimate system binary
  log_tampering_missing_lastlog    — lastlog file missing or zeroed (attacker covering tracks)
  log_tampering_missing_wtmp       — wtmp/btmp log deleted or tampered
  user_ssh_authorized_keys_*       — SSH authorized_keys anomalies (duplicates, new keys)
  file_suid_unexpected             — Unexpected SUID bit on file
  file_immutable_suspicious        — Suspicious immutable file attribute
  cron_*                           — Suspicious cron job entries
  kernel_module_*                  — Kernel module anomalies, LKM rootkit indicators
  recon_*                          — Reconnaissance/baseline data (always_pass, severity 1)

